  "summary": "This paper investigates how training-point allocation strategies interact with feature representations—specifically comparing 64-channel AlphaEarth Foundations (AEF) annual satellite embeddings (2020 v2.1) against 33-feature seasonal Sentinel-2 optical composites—for recovering small palustrine wetland patches (0.2–1 ha) across 64 study cores in four US National Wetlands Inventory (NWI) projects. Using a budget of 2,000 labeled pixels per fit (5 seeds) and evaluating patch recovery (>=50% of valid patch pixels predicted positive at a 5% reference-upland false-positive rate), the study compares uniform within-class area sampling against privileged 'oracle' patch-first sampling (selecting a mapped patch uniformly, then a pixel) and two-stage image-derived proposal/uncertainty sampling. In the planned 16-core test split, patch-first allocation increased small-patch recovery by 12.8 percentage points (pp) for AEF embeddings (44.8% to 57.6%) versus 1.2 pp for optical features (36.4% to 37.6%), yielding an 11.6 pp interaction even though aggregate pixel-level IoU and producer's accuracy remained virtually unchanged. However, extensive post-hoc exploratory checks (transparently reported alongside the planned protocol) substantially qualify this finding: across all four split rotations, both predictors benefited (8.4 pp for embeddings vs. 3.9 pp for optical; 4.5 pp interaction); optical maps touched more patches under a >=1-pixel overlap criterion; inverse-size loss weighting on fixed area-sampled points recovered over half the embedding gain without sampling additional patches; and the within-project benefit failed to generalize under whole-project geographic transfer (0.9 pp interaction at target-standardized thresholds, and -3.6 pp embedding gain when transferring validation thresholds due to severe cross-region calibration drift).",
